    "description": "The Temple of Aphrodite at Histria is a Greek sanctuary located within the sacred precinct (temenos) of ancient Histria, a Milesian colony on the western Black Sea coast in modern Romania. It was dedicated to Aphrodite, and it formed part of the city’s organized religious landscape.",
    "details": "<p>The Temple of Aphrodite at Histria was located in the southern sector of the city’s temenos, the sacred precinct of the settlement. Excavations beginning in 1963 revealed that the temple was constructed over an earlier oikos, preserving its general plan. The earliest phase dates to the late Archaic period, with ceramic evidence indicating a construction date around 540–530 BCE. The building reflects strong East Greek, particularly Samian, architectural influence. Constructed of limestone and limestone-sandstone, the temple was oriented approximately 215° S–SW / 15° N–NE and consisted of a nearly square naos and a deep pronaos in a prostyle tetrastyle arrangement. It stood on a stepped podium (crepis) without a stylobate, supported by a substructure (stereobate) of green schist and marble fragments placed directly on bedrock. The deep pronaos and plan parallels are comparable to East Greek sanctuaries such as those at Samos. Stratigraphic evidence records both construction and destruction. Successive layers of shell, clay, and limestone debris—likely from stone cutting—raised the pronaos floor by approximately 3–4 cm. Destruction by fire is indicated by burnt floor layers, particularly in the pronaos, along with associated deposits of terracotta roof tiles and collapsed architectural material. Architectural fragments include Ionic column bases, column shafts, and capitals, including an anta capital with egg-and-dart and anthemion decoration dated to ca. 500–480 BCE. The temple underwent multiple reconstruction phases, including structural reinforcements and several episodes in which floors were raised. The temple fell out of use by the mid-1st century BCE and was subsequently de-sacralized, with its materials reused in later construction.</p>",
